Do not carry the boot thread's stack into the first kernel thread

run-143 died on "Assertion failed: thread->kernel_stack == 0" (stack.c:232),
which is the precondition stack_alloc() checks before it hands a thread a
stack of its own.  The first thread could never satisfy it here: the port's
nio-register-boot-stack.patch records the loader's stack in init_thread, and
thread_create_internal() copies init_thread over the new thread wholesale,
because that is how the new thread inherits the boot CPU state.  The copy
carried kernel_stack along, so kernel_thread_create()'s stack_alloc() tripped
over its own precondition on the very first thread the kernel made.

init_thread keeps its value.  It is the boot thread, and the record of where
its frames live is worth keeping.  Only the copy is cleared, which is what
upstream does, where init_thread.kernel_stack is zero to begin with.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/thread.c b/osfmk/kern/thread.c
@@ -1360,6 +1360,15 @@
 #pragma clang diagnostic pop
 
 		/*
+		 * nio: init_thread.kernel_stack records the loader's stack, and the
+		 * copy above carried that into this thread.  stack_alloc() asserts
+		 * that kernel_stack is still zero before it hands the thread a stack
+		 * of its own, so clear it here.  Upstream this field is zero in
+		 * init_thread and the copy is harmless.
+		 */
+		new_thread->kernel_stack = 0;
+
+		/*
 		 * Make the ctid table functional
 		 */
 		ctid_table_init();
